<!-- From Archive M1 -->
### [M1-EXT-08] Dynamic Spatial Hash Cell Quadtree Subdivision Splitter
> **tags** · general
> **tl;dr** · Dynamic Spatial Hash Cell Quadtree Subdivision Splitter
> **ctx** · Dynamic Spatial Hash Cell Quadtree Subdivision Splitter
> **meta** · depends-on: - · depended-by: M1-EXT-09

##### Systems Touched
Cross-thread component visibility. Lets worker threads read entity state without the registry taking a lock, feeding [M1-EXT-01] queries and [M1-EXT-06] mutations.
##### Math
Double-buffered view epoch: readers see `epoch[read]`, writers publish to `epoch[1-read]` then flip `read ^= 1` under a seqlock. Version counter prevents torn reads.
##### Algorithm
1. Worker reads the stable epoch. 2. Main thread mutates the shadow buffer. 3. Flip epoch; subsequent worker reads see the new state. No per-read lock.
##### Examples
epoch_t cur = epoch[read];        // worker reads stable epoch, no lock
// main thread mutates shadow buffer epoch[1-read], then:
epoch[read ^= 1];                 // seqlock flip; next read sees new state
##### Failure Modes
- Epoch-flip contention: two writers publishing to epoch[1-read] in one frame corrupt the shadow buffer; serialize the flip with a single-writer mutex or CAS.
- Weak memory ordering: the flip must be std::atomic with acquire/release; a plain bool tears on weak-x86/ARM and lets a worker read a half-written buffer.
- Writer starvation: a hot main thread flipping every frame can starve a slow worker mid-read; a seqlock version-mismatch retry bounds this.

##### Player-Facing Impact
Invisible to players; maintains frame pacing under multi-threaded systems queries and guarantees deterministic entity-state reads across worker threads.


