##### Systems Touched
Scales difficulty parameters of all five minigames: tolerance band width (M9-EXT-30), loom length / labeling (M9-EXT-31), pin-set window (M9-EXT-32), trace tolerance (M9-EXT-33), grab-angle window (M9-EXT-34). XP awarded per successful interaction (more for cleaner successes).

##### Math
Proficiency level `L∈[0..5]` per skill. Window scale `w(L) = w₀ · (1 + 0.15·L)`. Noise scale `n(L) = n₀ · (1 - 0.12·L)`. XP to next level `X(L) = 50·L²`. Cross-skill: a master welder is not automatically a master thief.

##### Algorithm
Each minigame reads its skill level from M8-EXT-18 at open. Higher level → wider success windows, more visual/audio tells (easier reads), less noise on fumble. New players get the hard, quiet version; veterans get forgiving, legible versions — but context risk (fuel spark, guard cone) is never removed, so mastery is about efficiency and safety, not immunity.

##### Examples
- New player hotwires (M9-EXT-31): long unlabeled loom, easy to spark, draws zombies. After 20 successful hotwires (M8-EXT-18 XP): loom shortens, live wires glow, sparks rare.
- Master lockpick (M9-EXT-32, L5): 6-pin safe opens in 15s, near-silent; still risks the relock pin if they pause.

