### [M2-EXT-124]

> **tags** · m2.9, system
> **tl;dr** · Sleep-Deprivation Hallucination Drift — M2-EXT-124.
> **ctx** · M2.9-EXT-11 — M2.9 fatigue/wakefulness scalar.
> **meta** · depends-on: — · depended-by: -
Sleep-Deprivation Hallucination Drift

##### Systems Touched

M2.9 survival/physiology state (a `fatigue`/wakefulness scalar alongside `[M2.9-EXT-07]`'s hydration-exertion and `[M2.9-EXT-08]`'s BCS), M4.5/M1 G-buffer post-processing (feeds a subtle distortion term into the existing tonemapping/post pass, reusing the same attachment path as `[M4.5-EXT-02]`/`[M4.5-EXT-03]` rather than adding a new pass).

##### Math

A single hallucination-drift scalar `H` follows an Ornstein–Uhlenbeck (mean-reverting) random walk, so it wanders continuously instead of jittering per-frame or ramping monotonically:

`dH = θ·(μ − H)·dt + σ·√dt·𝒩(0,1)`

where `μ = 0` (drift reverts to no-distortion), `θ` is the reversion rate, and the volatility `σ` is scaled by sleep debt: `σ = σ_base · SleepDebt`, `SleepDebt ∈ [0,1]`. The output feeding the shader is clamped to a deliberately small envelope: `H_shader = clamp(H, −H_max, +H_max)` with `H_max` kept subtle.

##### Algorithm

Sleep debt accumulates while the player stays awake past a rested threshold and drains with sleep. It scales only the *volatility* `σ` of an Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process — a rested player has `σ≈0` so `H` sits at zero (no effect at all); a severely sleep-deprived player has high `σ`, so `H` wanders further from zero before the mean-reversion term pulls it back. The scalar `H` is fed as a small magnitude into the existing G-buffer post-processing distortion (a gentle screen-space warp / periphery shimmer), reusing the same persistent post attachment the retinal-adaptation effects already own, so no new render pass is added. The magnitude is intentionally kept subtle — this is unease-at-the-edges, a periphery that doesn't quite hold still, not a psychedelic full-screen warp; the `H_max` clamp guarantees it can never blow out even if `σ` is misconfigured. Because the OU step is a plain scalar update, it costs nothing on the sim side and integrates in the fixed-timestep loop.

##### Examples

```cpp
// Ornstein-Uhlenbeck mean-reverting drift; volatility scaled by sleep debt so a rested player sees
// nothing and a deprived player sees subtle wander. Output clamped to a deliberately small envelope.
float StepHallucinationDrift(float h, float sleepDebt01, float theta, float sigmaBase,
                             float hMax, float dt, float gaussianSample /* ~N(0,1) */) {
    float sigma = sigmaBase * std::clamp(sleepDebt01, 0.0f, 1.0f);
    h += theta * (0.0f - h) * dt + sigma * std::sqrt(dt) * gaussianSample; // mu = 0
    return std::clamp(h, -hMax, hMax); // fed to the G-buffer distortion post pass
}
```

##### Failure Modes

SleepDebt not clamped → σ explodes, H saturates the clamp every frame (constant distortion).
H not persisted on save/load → hallucination state lost or corrupted across sessions.
theta = 0 (no reversion) → H random-walks unbounded before clamp, flickers at envelope edge.


##### Player-Facing Impact

Pushing days without sleep makes the edges of your vision subtly unreliable — a faint, wandering shimmer and warp at the periphery that never quite resolves, growing as exhaustion deepens and vanishing once you rest. It reads as creeping unease rather than a gimmicky screen effect, and it gives sleep an actual felt cost instead of a hidden stat.

