### [M5-EXT-20] Anatomical Hit-Volume SDF Gradients

> **tags** · SDF
> **tl;dr** · Declares the base "Anatomical Hit-Volume SDF Gradients" bullet. Consumes [M5-EXT-19]'s Master Skeleton pose data (skinned vertex positions feed the SDF's zer...
> **ctx** · Anatomical Hit-Volume SDF Gradients -- Declares the base "Anatomical Hit-Volume SDF Gradients" bullet. Consumes [M5-EXT-19]'s Master Skeleton pose data (skinned vertex positions feed the SDF's zero-crossing surface) and feeds M2.9's ballistic damage resolution — hit location and penetration depth are read off the gradient rather than a raw mesh raycast.
> **meta** · depends-on: M5-EXT-19 · depended-by: -

##### Systems Touched
Declares the base "Anatomical Hit-Volume SDF Gradients" bullet. Consumes `[M5-EXT-19]`'s Master Skeleton pose data (skinned vertex positions feed the SDF's zero-crossing surface) and feeds M2.9's ballistic damage resolution — hit location and penetration depth are read off the gradient rather than a raw mesh raycast.

##### Math
Each anatomical region (skull, torso, limb) is represented as a signed distance field; a hit's damage multiplier and exit-wound depth come from the gradient at the impact point: `Depth = |Φ(p)| / |∇Φ(p)|`, where `Φ` is the SDF value and `∇Φ` its normalized gradient direction.

##### Algorithm
Rather than raycasting the full skinned mesh per shot (expensive at horde density), each zombie carries a small set of primitive SDF volumes (capsules/ellipsoids) rigged to the Master Skeleton's bones, updated once per animation tick alongside `[M5-EXT-19]`'s pose. A hit tests against this lightweight SDF stack instead of triangle geometry: the gradient at the query point gives both the surface normal (for spatter direction) and penetration depth (for through-and-through vs. stopping-power resolution) in one evaluation.

##### Examples
-

##### Failure Modes
-

##### Player-Facing Impact
Headshots and limb hits register consistently even mid-animation (staggering, lunging, ragdolling) without expensive per-triangle raycasts, and bullet penetration depth reads correctly against actual body mass rather than a flat hitbox.


