<!-- From Archive M8 -->
### [M8-EXT-51] Dynamic Open-World Horde Migration

> **tags** · combat, ai, world
> **tl;dr** · No fixed calendar. Horde threat emerges continuously from world-state signals (noise, scent, screamers, combat, time/weather). Wandering horde entities migrate across the map drawn by additive attraction signals. Escalation = probability stack: Idle → Scout → Siege → Breach. Sanctuary mechanic protects player hubs behaviorally (not temporally).
> **ctx** · Dynamic Horde Migration -- M8/M5/M10. Replaces scheduled "blood moon" with emergent signal-driven migration. Reuses M5-EXT-14 (hearing-cone), M5-EXT-65 (scent), M5-EXT-64 (sleeper/screamer), M10 weather/season, M8-EXT-53 (sanctuary). 7DTD fixed Blood Moon is exploitable (cheese day 6); SoD2 Plague Hearts prove emergent location-based escalation; Urban Strife proves alert propagation > central command.
> **meta** · depends-on: [M5-EXT-14], [M5-EXT-65], [M5-EXT-64], [M10], [M8-EXT-53] · depended-by: [M8-EXT-54], [M8-EXT-59]

##### Systems Touched
Wandering horde entities (persistent groups) migrate across the map drawn by emergent attraction signals. No timer, no calendar. Signals are additive: player noise (M5-EXT-14 hearing-cone radius), scent trails (M5-EXT-65 pheromone), screamer alerts (M5-EXT-64 sleeper), faction combat (gunfire/explosions), time-of-day + weather (night/rain increases boldness/detection). Escalation = probability stack of active signals: Idle (baseline wander) → Scout (1-2 signals, perimeter probes) → Siege (3+ signals, directed movement, structure testing) → Breach (sustained Siege + player in structure, interior infiltration). Sanctuary: claimed player bases (M8-EXT-53) emit deterrent field reducing wandering-horde pathing weight 60% within 100m; does NOT prevent Siege/Breach if player generates strong signals inside.

##### Math
```
base_density = zone_base + (days_survived * 0.1) + (player_level * 0.5)
signal_bonus = sum(active_signal_strength)  // noise, scent, screamers, combat
horde_size = base_density * (1 + signal_bonus * 0.3)  // capped by zone max
escalation_tier = clamp(floor(signal_bonus), 0, 3)  // 0=Idle, 1=Scout, 2=Siege, 3=Breach
```

##### Algorithm
1. Maintain persistent horde entities with positions/migration vectors. 2. Each tick, evaluate active attraction signals at entity positions. 3. Compute signal_bonus and escalation_tier. 4. Migration vector steers toward highest signal gradient; escalation_tier gates behavior set (wander/scout/siege/breach). 5. Sanctuary field applies negative weight to player-base cells in pathing. 6. Horde size sampled from zone density + signal bonus at spawn time.

##### Examples
- Player fires unsuppressed rifle in quiet zone → noise signal spikes → nearby horde escalates to Scout, probes perimeter.
- Player camps near fresh kill → scent signal persists → horde lingers at Scout/Siege boundary.
- Screamer screams in dense zone → alert propagates through network → multiple hordes escalate to Siege simultaneously.
- Player inside claimed base at night with rain → high base signal but sanctuary field active → wandering hordes diverted; only Siege/Breach-tier hordes press.

##### Failure Modes
- Signal stacking creates runaway escalation in dense combat → cap signal_bonus and escalation_tier.
- Sanctuary trivializes horde threat → sanctuary only reduces wandering pathing weight, not Siege/Breach commitment.
- Horde entities bunch at signal sources → add separation force to migration vector.

##### Player-Facing Impact
Horde threat feels like a living ecosystem, not a calendar event. No "day 6 cheese." Tension is always present, scaling with player activity. A quiet playstyle sees Idle/Scout; aggressive play draws Siege/Breach. Sanctuary rewards investment without making bases invulnerable.


